+### Handle page-limit API errors 
+
+When a page limit applies, Stripe estimates the total number of pages in the final evidence PDF as part of an [API evidence update](https://docs.stripe.com/api/disputes/update.md). The estimate includes text and uploaded files already saved on the dispute, with your updates applied.
+
+If the updated evidence exceeds the applicable limit, Stripe returns an HTTP `400` response with `error.type` set to `invalid_request_error` and `error.code` set to `dispute_evidence_page_limit_exceeded`. This check applies whether `submit` is `true` or `false`.
+
+Handle `error.code` programmatically. Use `error.message` to understand the applicable page limit and the breakdown of pages from text and uploaded files. Shorten the text or reduce the number of pages in uploaded files, then retry the request.
+
+For example, an error response includes these properties:
+
+```json
+{
+  "error": {
+    "code": "dispute_evidence_page_limit_exceeded",
+    "message": "Your dispute evidence exceeds the 17-page compiled PDF limit. Your text fields account for 0 page(s) and your file uploads account for 36 page(s). Shorten text fields or reduce pages in your uploaded files to stay within the limit. https://docs.stripe.com/disputes/best-practices#limit-file-length",
+    "type": "invalid_request_error"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Handle this error using `error.code`. Don’t extract page limits or page counts from `error.message` for programmatic validation. The limit and page counts in this example are illustrative. The applicable limit can vary by dispute.
 
